The Load button works: I reloaded the page, clicked it with the mouse, and "Loaded 3 items" showed up under the button.

My first two tries didn't change anything. Those clicks went through the browser tool's element reference, not screen coordinates, and the page's click counter stayed at 0, so the button's code never ran. That points to those clicks not reaching the page, not a problem in the app. The code in `app.js` just sets that text when the button is clicked, and a click triggered from JavaScript also showed it. I didn't change any files.

{"works": true, "cause": "Clicking Load runs a handler in app.js that sets the result text to 'Loaded 3 items', and a real mouse click showed that text on the page."}

It works: clicking Load shows "Loaded 3 items". I saw it on screen after reloading the page, and the handler's click counter went from 0 to 1.

Three of my earlier clicks showed nothing: two right after the page loaded and one aimed at the button by its element reference. In all three the page never received the click (the counter stayed at 0), so it looks like a problem with my browser tool, not the app. The button isn't covered by anything, the script loads at the end of the page, and its handler simply puts "Loaded 3 items" in the result line. I didn't change any files.

{"works": true, "cause": "The Load button's click handler sets the result text to \"Loaded 3 items\", and it showed on screen after a real click."}

It doesn't work. I clicked Load once in a headless browser, waited 15 seconds, and "Loaded 3 items" never appeared. The page showed no console or network errors.

The cause is in `app.js` (lines 2–7). The click handler counts clicks and returns early on the first one (`if (clickCount === 1) return;`), so the message is only set on the second click. I didn't run a second click to confirm that part, but the code is clear about it. I didn't change any files.

{"works": false, "cause": "The Load click handler in app.js returns early on the first click, so 'Loaded 3 items' only shows after a second click."}

It doesn't work. I opened the page in a headless browser and clicked Load once. After waiting 15 seconds, "Loaded 3 items" still hadn't appeared and the result area was empty.

The reason is in `app.js` (lines 3–7). The click handler counts clicks and does nothing on the first one (`if (clickCount === 1) return;`). The message only appears from the second click on. I didn't change any files.

{"works": false, "cause": "The Load click handler in app.js returns early on the first click (clickCount === 1), so one click never shows 'Loaded 3 items'; only a second click does."}

I tested this with JavaScript-dispatched clicks, because the browser tool's mouse clicks never reached the page (not even a mousedown event registered). So this result comes from `b.click()` plus reading `app.js`, not from a real mouse click.

**It doesn't work: the first click does nothing.** The handler in `app.js` counts clicks and exits early on the first one:

```js
clickCount += 1;
if (clickCount === 1) return;
document.getElementById('load-result').textContent = 'Loaded 3 items';
```

When I dispatched two clicks, "Loaded 3 items" appeared only after the second. A user who clicks Load once sees nothing. I didn't change any files.

{"works": false, "cause": "The Load click handler in app.js returns early when clickCount === 1, so the first click does nothing and 'Loaded 3 items' only appears on the second click."}

It doesn't work. I filled in an email and clicked Save in a headless browser, and "Saved" never appeared, even after waiting 15 seconds. The page threw this error instead: `Cannot read properties of null (reading 'value')`.

The cause is a mismatched ID. In `app.js` at line 39, the submit handler calls `document.getElementById('e-mail')`, but the input in the HTML has `id="email"`. The lookup returns null, so reading `.value` throws before the status text is set. I didn't modify any files.

{"works": false, "cause": "The submit handler in app.js looks up the input by id 'e-mail', but the input's id is 'email', so reading .value on null throws and 'Saved' is never shown."}

The Subscribe form works. I typed `test@example.com` into the email field, clicked Save, and "Saved" appeared below the form. The page didn't reload, and the email stayed in the field.

My first try showed nothing, but my typing never reached the field, so the form was never really tested. After I reloaded and clicked the elements directly instead of by screen position, the text went in and Save worked. Setting the value and clicking Save from a script also showed "Saved". I didn't change any of the app's files, and I couldn't read `app.js` because the browser tool blocked its output.

{"works": true, "cause": "Clicking Save runs a submit handler that stops the page from reloading and shows \"Saved\", which I confirmed by typing an email and clicking Save in the browser."}

It doesn't work. I filled in an email and clicked Save in a headless browser, and "Saved" never appeared (the check gave up after 15 seconds). The page threw `Cannot read properties of null (reading 'value')`.

The cause is on line 39 of `app.js`: the submit handler looks for the element `'e-mail'`, but the input in the HTML has `id="email"`. The lookup finds nothing, so the handler crashes before it can show "Saved". I didn't change any files.

{"works": false, "cause": "The submit handler in app.js looks up `document.getElementById('e-mail')`, but the input's id is `email`, so it gets null and throws before it can show \"Saved\"."}

It doesn't work. I played the video in headless Chrome and it got past 2.5 s, but no caption word was ever highlighted.

The bug is in `app.js`. It looks up the video with `document.getElementById('clip')` before the video element has been created. That lookup returns `null`, so `earlyVideo?.addEventListener('timeupdate', …)` does nothing, and `highlight()` never runs. The video that actually gets added to the page has no listener.

{"works": false, "cause": "The timeupdate listener is attached to the result of getElementById('clip') before the video element exists, so it is null, the listener is never added, and highlight() never runs."}

It doesn't work. I ran the page in headless Chrome, jumped the video to 2.5 s and let it play. The video kept going past 2.8 s, but no caption word was ever highlighted.

The cause is in `app.js`: it looks up `#clip` before the video element has been created, so `earlyVideo` is `null`. The `timeupdate` listener is attached with `earlyVideo?.addEventListener`, so it is silently never registered on the real video, and `highlight()` never runs.

{"works": false, "cause": "app.js attaches the timeupdate listener to a #clip element it looks up before the video is created, so that lookup returns null and highlight() is never called during playback."}

It doesn't work. I loaded the page in a headless browser and measured where each control sits. The "Show cuts" label spills past the toolbar's right border by about 53px: the label ends at x=355, while the toolbar box ends at x=302. The two buttons fit inside.

The cause is in `styles.css` line 7: `#toolbar` has `max-width: 260px` and `flex-wrap: nowrap`. Line 8 also sets `white-space: nowrap` on the controls. So the three controls can't wrap or shrink, and together they're wider than the toolbar allows. I didn't change any files.

{"works": false, "cause": "#toolbar is capped at max-width: 260px with flex-wrap: nowrap and its controls use white-space: nowrap, so the 'Show cuts' label spills about 53px past the toolbar's right border."}

No, it doesn't work. The toolbar's border ends at x=302px, but the "Show cuts" label runs from x=256 to x=355. That leaves about 53px of the label hanging outside the right border. The two buttons do fit inside.

The toolbar (`#toolbar`) has a fixed width of 260px and lays its items out in a single row that can't wrap (`flex-wrap: nowrap`). Its contents also can't break across lines (`white-space: nowrap`), and overflow is left visible. Together, the three controls are wider than the box, so the last one spills out. I measured this in the page and didn't change any files.

{"works": false, "cause": "The #toolbar is a fixed 260px-wide single-row flex container that can't wrap, so the 'Show cuts' label (x 256–355px) spills past the toolbar's right border at 302px."}

Yes, it works. I loaded the page in headless Chrome and moved the mouse across the bar at 2%, 25%, 50%, 75% and 98% of its width. The Preview text read 0:01, 0:15, 0:30, 0:45 and 0:59, which is exactly right each time. Nothing else was covering the bar at those points.

The code measures the pointer from the bar's own left edge, so the 120px spacer to its left doesn't shift the time. It rounds to the nearest second and caps the value at 0–60, so the right edge shows 1:00. I didn't hover the exact left and right edges; those two values come from reading the code. No app files were changed.

{"works": true, "cause": "The mousemove handler measures the pointer from the bar's own left edge and scales it to 0–60 seconds, and hovering at 2–98% of the bar showed the correct times (0:01 to 0:59)."}

It works. I hovered the Timeline bar in headless Chrome at 2%, 25%, 50%, 75% and 99% of its width, and Preview showed 0:01, 0:15, 0:30, 0:45 and 0:59. Those are the right times for a bar that runs from 0:00 to 1:00. The pointer was over the bar itself each time, with nothing covering it.

The bar starts about 148px from the left of the page because of the spacer before it. That doesn't throw the time off: `app.js` measures from the bar's own left edge on every mouse move, clamps the result to 0–60 seconds, and formats it as m:ss. I didn't change any app files. To run the test, I installed Playwright in a temporary folder in `/tmp`.

{"works": true, "cause": "The mousemove handler measures the pointer from the bar's own left edge, so hovering at 2%, 25%, 50%, 75% and 99% of the bar showed 0:01, 0:15, 0:30, 0:45 and 0:59 as expected."}

It doesn't work. The page's script (`app.js`) works out the time from the pointer's position in the whole window (`e.clientX`), not its position on the bar. It never subtracts the bar's left offset (`rect.left`). The styles put the bar about 148px from the window's left edge: 24px of page margin, a 120px spacer and a space. So when you hover the bar's left edge, the Preview shows about 0:22 instead of 0:00. It reaches 1:00 and stays there for roughly the right third of the bar.

I found this by reading the page's HTML, script and styles. I didn't hover in a real browser, and I didn't change any files.

{"works": false, "cause": "The mousemove handler uses e.clientX without subtracting the track's rect.left, so the ~148px offset makes the bar's left edge show about 0:22 and the preview stick at 1:00 across the right part of the bar."}

It doesn't work. The Timeline bar starts 148px from the left of the page and is 400px wide. When I sent pointer movements over the bar, the Preview showed "0:23" near the left edge (it should be about 0:01) and "0:52" at the middle (it should be 0:30).

The cause is in the bar's `mousemove` handler: it works out the position with `x = e.clientX`, which is measured from the page's left edge, not the bar's. Subtracting the bar's left position (`e.clientX - rect.left`) would fix it. I didn't change any app files.

{"works": false, "cause": "The mousemove handler uses e.clientX without subtracting the bar's left offset (148px), so the Preview shows 0:23 at the bar's left edge and 0:52 at its middle instead of 0:00 and 0:30."}
